Schema width test: 64 FLOAT32 embedding dims + WORKER_ID + TS = 66 columns (A1 -> B, 1000 rows)
Date: 2026-11-09, local Mac (Docker Desktop, Apple Silicon), pinned nes-worker digest sha256:314e7861...

VERDICT: Yes. NebulaStream handles 64-dim embeddings (66 columns) with no data loss and exact values.

Test                              | Rows at B   | Embeddings vs. input  | SCORE vs. Python
1 passthrough (66 cols)           | 1000/1000   | max err 0 (exact)     | n/a
2 SQL dot-product SCORE, default  | 1000/1000   | max err 0 (exact)     | max err 5.3e-07
3 SCORE forced onto B (max_ops=0) | 1000/1000   | max err 0 (exact)     | max err 5.3e-07
(0 missing rows in all tests; output has 66 cols in test 1, 67 with SCORE in tests 2/3.)

Placement:
- Test 1: the plain-field projection is optimised away; A1 = SOURCE -> SINK(NETWORK), B = SOURCE(NETWORK) -> SINK.
- Test 2 (default): the SCORE projection runs on worker-a1 (computed at the edge, then shipped to B).
- Test 3 (A1 max_operators=0): the SCORE projection runs on worker-b, as intended.

Errors/warnings: only the known benign ones, all on worker-a1 (35 lines over 3 runs):
"Channel ... was rejected" warnings at startup, and teardown [E] lines
